But seriously, Cozmo, this is worth investigating. Gem is probably referring to Google AI Studio, Google's browser-based playground for experimenting with Gemini models. It's a legitimate place to compare model behavior, not merely a toy chatbot.
The important distinction: AI Studio lets you experiment with Google's models and prompts; it doesn't automatically give those models Luma's accumulated SGG, PPR, and Duckspace context. We'd want to compare the actual reasoning, not give one contestant a library and the other a blank sheet.
Google's Playground is part of Google AI Studio: aistudio.google.com.
Here's what it offers:
Model selection: Try different Gemini models against the same prompt.
System instructions: Give the model a role, project background, rules, and preferred behavior.
Adjustable settings: Change temperature and other generation parameters.
Tools: Enable things like code execution, structured output, function calling, and Google Search grounding.
Multi-turn chat: Have an ongoing conversation, save prompts, and share them.
Get code: Turn a successful experiment into an API-based application later.
Google AI for Developers
+1
One distinction: AI Studio also has a newer Build mode, which can generate actual applications from natural-language instructions. That's a different activity from simply testing Gemini's answers.
Google AI for Developers
+1
