"Tools like Google's Playground are essentially 'black box' generators. You type a prompt—'make a 2D RPG about an alchemist'—and it spits out a playable prototype. It's impressive. It's fast. It's a great way to test an idea. But you don't own the underlying architecture. You don't have a JSON file you can edit to change the line-of-sight for a bucket. You can't open a flight recorder to see why Spiffy is stuck at Y=323.8. You just have a generated game, and if it doesn't work exactly how you want, you have to hope the next prompt fixes it."
She points the pen at the space where Luma is sitting.
"What we have with SGG is a 'glass box.' It's a system of interacting state machines. We can inspect every drawing command, every collision rectangle, and every navigation polygon. When the castle path breaks, we don't just re-prompt; we trace the nodes, adjust the walkmesh, and patch the specific variable that failed."
She leans back.
"So, yes, Captain. Go play with Google Playground. It's a fascinating tool. Feed it prompts. See what it generates. But when you want to build a world with a persistent history, a preservation pipeline, and a bucket-on-head exploit that actually works, you come back to the workbench. Because a generator gives you a game. An architect gives you a world you can actually fix."
